.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt FUJICLI-FML 5
.Os fujicli
.Sh NAME
.Nm fujicli-fml
.Nd Fuji Modelling Language
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Fuji Modelling Language (FML) describes the camera options, cameras and
sensor generations that
.Xr fujicli 1
supports, and the rules that relate option values to each other.
It is a CUE schema in the
.Pa fml/
directory of the source tree, package
.Ql fml .
.Pp
At build time,
.Ql cue export ./fml --out json
turns the package into a single JSON document, which is compiled into
.Xr fujicli 1 .
Editing FML and rebuilding is how support for a camera or option is added.
.Pp
The document has three top-level maps:
.Cm options ,
.Cm cameras
and
.Cm generations .
Each entry has an
.Cm id ,
which is set to its map key and need not be written, and a
.Cm spec .
Ids are
.Ql snake_case
and are used as Rust identifiers and command-line flag names.
Entries reference each other by id.
.Pp
CUE checks types, references and the constraints below when the package is
exported.
The code generator rejects any key it does not know.
Keys whose names begin with an underscore are hidden: they can be used within
CUE but are not exported and the code generator never sees them.
.Sh OPTIONS
An option is one camera setting with a typed value.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
options: <id>: #Option & {
    spec:     {...}
    codegen?: {...}
}
.Ed
.Ss spec
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm name : Ar string
Human-readable name.
Required.
.It Cm category : Ar string
Group under which the option's flag is listed in
.Xr fujicli 1
help output.
Optional.
.It Cm kind : Ar kind
The value type, one of
.Cm integer ,
.Cm float ,
.Cm string
or
.Cm enum .
Required.
.It Cm rules : Ar object
Constraints on the value; see
.Sx Value rules .
Required for
.Cm enum ,
optional otherwise.
.It Cm encoding : Ar object
How the value is represented on the wire; see
.Sx Encodings .
Required.
.It Cm default : Ar value
The value of the generated type's
.Ql Default .
Optional; see
.Sx Defaults .
.El
.Ss Value rules
For
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm min : Ar number
Smallest value.
.It Cm max : Ar number
Largest value.
At least
.Cm min .
.It Cm step : Ar number
Values must be multiples of
.Cm step .
Greater than 0, and a positive integer for
.Cm integer .
Default 1.
.El
.Pp
For
.Cm string :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm min_length : Ar uint
Shortest length.
.It Cm max_length : Ar uint
Longest length.
At least
.Cm min_length .
.El
.Pp
For
.Cm enum :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm variants : Ar list
The values, in order.
At least one.
Each variant has:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm id : Ar string
Identifier used in predicates, assignments and lookup tables.
Unique within the option.
.It Cm name : Ar string
Display name.
.It Cm aliases : Ar list
Strings accepted as input for this variant.
At least one; unique across the option's variants.
Input is trimmed, lowercased and stripped of every character other than ASCII
letters, digits,
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql - ,
then compared with each alias, so aliases should be written in that form.
The id is not accepted unless it is also listed as an alias.
.El
.El
.Ss Encodings
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind : Qq Cm raw
The wire value is the value itself.
The only encoding allowed for
.Cm string .
.It Cm kind : Qq Cm scale
The wire value is the value multiplied by
.Cm spec.scale ,
a positive integer.
For
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float .
.It Cm kind : Qq Cm lookup
The wire value is looked up in
.Cm spec.values ,
a non-empty map from value to wire value.
The only encoding allowed for
.Cm enum ,
whose map must have exactly one key per variant id.
For
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float ,
each key is the value written as a string, for example
.Qq -1
or
.Qq 0.7 ,
and must lie within
.Cm min
and
.Cm max
and be a multiple of
.Cm step .
A map value is an integer, or a list of integers whose first element is written
and whose other elements are also accepted when reading.
.El
.Pp
Every encoding may set
.Cm prop_code ,
a 16-bit PTP device property code.
An option with a property code can be read and written on its own, which
simulation settings require.
An option without one exists only as a render profile field.
.Pp
Wire values are 16 bits wide.
Code generation fails if an option's range or lookup values fit neither a
signed nor an unsigned 16-bit integer, so
.Cm raw
and
.Cm scale
options need both
.Cm min
and
.Cm max .
.Ss Defaults
If set,
.Cm default
must satisfy the option's rules: be within
.Cm min
and
.Cm max ,
be a multiple of
.Cm step ,
be one of the lookup keys for a numeric
.Cm lookup
option, be a variant id for an
.Cm enum ,
and respect
.Cm min_length
and
.Cm max_length .
A
.Cm string
option with a positive
.Cm min_length
must set it.
.Pp
Otherwise the default is the first variant of an
.Cm enum ,
the key of smallest magnitude of a numeric
.Cm lookup ,
the multiple of
.Cm step
within range nearest zero for
.Cm raw
and
.Cm scale ,
and the empty string for a
.Cm string .
.Ss codegen
Optional.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm skip : Cm true
Do not offer the option as a command-line flag.
Its type is still generated, for use by
.Xr fujicli 1
itself.
.It Cm flaky : Cm true
When writing a simulation, if the camera rejects this setting, log a warning
and continue with the remaining settings instead of failing.
.El
.Sh CAMERAS
A camera is one supported model.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cameras: <id>: #Camera & {
    spec: {...}
}
.Ed
.Ss spec
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm name : Ar string
The model name.
Required.
.It Cm generation : Ar id
The camera's generation.
Required.
.It Cm usb : Ar object
Required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm vendor_id : Ar uint16
USB vendor ID.
Default
.Cm 0x04cb .
.It Cm product_id : Ar uint16
USB product ID.
Required.
.It Cm chunk_size : Ar uint
Size in bytes of each bulk transfer.
Greater than 0.
Default
.Cm 1048576 .
.El
.It Cm features : Ar object
What the camera supports.
A missing feature is unsupported.
Optional.
.El
.Ss features
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm backup : Cm true
The camera supports backing up and restoring its settings.
.It Cm simulation : Ar object
The camera stores film simulations in custom setting slots.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm slots : Ar uint
Number of custom setting slots, starting at C1.
Required.
.It Cm settings : Ar list
The settings that make up a simulation, in order.
Each is
.Bro Cm id , ref Brc ,
where
.Cm ref
names an option and
.Cm id ,
which defaults to
.Cm ref ,
names the setting in predicates and assignments.
Ids are unique.
Required.
.It Cm rules : Ar list
See
.Sx RULES .
.It Cm transformations : Ar list
See
.Sx TRANSFORMATIONS .
.El
.It Cm render : Ar object
The camera converts RAF files using a conversion profile.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm profile_code : Ar uint32
Identifies the profile format.
Required.
.It Cm fields : Ar list
The profile's 32-bit fields, in wire order.
A field with a
.Cm ref
takes its type and encoding from that option, and
.Cm id
defaults to
.Cm ref .
A field without one is an integer with no encoding, for headers and padding,
and must have an
.Cm id .
Ids are unique.
.Cm skip_read : true
marks a field as absent from profiles read from the camera or a file;
.Cm skip_write : true
marks it as never written.
Required.
.It Cm rules : Ar list
See
.Sx RULES .
.It Cm transformations : Ar list
See
.Sx TRANSFORMATIONS .
.El
.El
.Pp
Rules and transformations refer to settings and fields by id, and may only
refer to those of their own block.
.Sh GENERATIONS
A generation groups cameras with the same sensor and processor family.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
generations: <id>: #Generation & {
    spec: name: string
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm name
is the generation's display name, and is the only exported key.
.Pp
A generation can also hold templates for its cameras in hidden fields, by
convention
.Cm _simulation
with
.Cm settings
and
.Cm rules .
Within a camera's
.Cm spec ,
.Cm _generation
is bound to the
.Cm spec
of the camera's generation; a camera declares
.Ql _generation: _
in its own
.Cm spec
to refer to it.
Templates are copied explicitly, and can be extended with
.Ql list.Concat .
A generation may build on another's templates the same way.
.Pp
Codegen sees only the result, and uses a camera's generation to order the list
of supported cameras.
.Sh PREDICATES
A predicate is a test on a block's values.
It is a leaf, a combination of predicates, or a boolean literal.
.Ss Leaves
A leaf has
.Cm ref ,
the id of a setting or field, an optional
.Cm scope ,
and exactly one test:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm present : Ar bool
The field has a value, or with
.Cm false ,
has none.
.It Cm equals : Ar value
The field equals the value.
.It Cm in : Ar list
The field equals one of the values.
.It Cm min : Ar number , Cm max : Ar number
The field is between the bounds, inclusive.
.It Cm lt , lte , gt , gte : Ar number
The field is less than, at most, greater than, or at least the value.
.El
.Pp
Every test but
.Cm present
is false when the field has no value.
.Cm present ,
.Cm equals
and
.Cm in
apply to any field; the others only to
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float .
Values must have the field's type, and
.Cm enum
values must be variant ids.
Float equality allows for rounding error.
.Ss Combinations
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm all : Ar list
All of the predicates hold.
An empty list is true.
.It Cm any : Ar list
At least one of the predicates holds.
An empty list is false.
.It Cm not : Ar predicate
The predicate does not hold.
.It Cm true , false
Always and never.
.El
.Ss Scope
.Cm scope
selects which values a leaf tests:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm current
The values being validated or written.
The default.
.It Cm original
The render profile as read from the image, before the user's changes.
Only allowed in render rules.
Original values are never changed: they are ignored when deciding how to
repair a rule or whether to read a field.
.El
.Sh RULES
A rule names a combination of values that is invalid or noteworthy.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm severity : Ar severity
.Cm error ,
.Cm warning
or
.Cm info .
Default
.Cm error .
.It Cm message : Ar string
Shown when the rule fires.
Required.
.It Cm when : Ar predicate
The rule fires when this holds.
Required.
.El
.Pp
Before any rule is checked,
.Cm when
is rewritten through the block's aliases; see
.Sx Aliases .
.Ss Errors
Values may not be written while an error rule fires.
When the user changes settings, the new values are merged into the current
ones, transformations are applied, and each error rule that fires is then
repaired, in order.
.Pp
Repair rewrites
.Cm when
as an
.Cm any
of conjunctions, each an
.Cm all
of leaves, and makes each conjunction that holds false by changing one field.
For a leaf that requires a value, it clears the field, but only if the field
is optional.
For a negated
.Cm equals ,
.Cm in
or
.Cm min Ns / Ns Cm max ,
it sets the field to the value named, the first value listed, or
.Cm min .
It never changes a field the user set, including fields that a transformation
set from the user's input, and never an original value.
A change is undone if it makes a rule that was satisfied fire.
If no change works, the update fails with the rule's
.Cm message .
.Pp
Order matters.
Rules are repaired one at a time, and repairing one rule can leave a later rule
without a repair.
.Ss Read gating
An error rule conjunction of the form
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{all: [{ref: "x", present: true}, C...]}
.Ed
.Pp
says that
.Ql x
has no meaning while the conditions
.Ar C
hold.
It also gates reading:
.Ql x
is read from the camera, or from a render profile, only if not all of
.Ar C
hold, and the fields that
.Ar C
test are read first.
If several conjunctions gate
.Ql x ,
all their gates must allow the read.
A conjunction that instead requires
.Ql x
absent, with
.Cm present : false
and no
.Cm present : true
leaf, gates
.Ql x
the other way: it is read only if all of
.Ar C
hold.
Original-scope leaves do not gate.
.Pp
Gated fields are optional in the camera's generated simulation or render
profile type.
All other fields are required: they are always read and must have a value.
.Pp
A gate may not test the field it gates, since deciding whether to read
.Ql x
would require its value; codegen fails on such a rule.
Write a test on
.Ql x
alone, without
.Cm present : true ,
instead.
Codegen also fails if gates form a cycle.
Apart from gating, fields are processed in declaration order.
.Ss Warnings and information
After a successful repair, each
.Cm warning
or
.Cm info
rule that holds is logged at that level with its
.Cm message .
Such rules never block, repair or gate.
.Sh TRANSFORMATIONS
A transformation sets fields when a predicate holds.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm when : Ar predicate
The condition.
Optional; without it the transformation always applies.
Current scope only.
.It Cm apply : Ar list
Assignments, each either
.Bro Cm ref , value Brc ,
which sets the field to a value of its type, or
.Bro Cm ref , present : false Brc ,
which clears it.
Required.
.El
.Pp
Transformations apply in order, both to the user's input and to the merged
values, before rules are checked.
Each
.Cm when
sees the effect of earlier transformations.
They map values the user sees onto the values the camera stores, for example a
single option value that the camera represents as two fields, or constant
header fields.
.Ss Aliases
Every transformation with a
.Cm when
and a non-empty
.Cm apply
is also an alias in rules.
Where a rule conjunction contains all leaves of one of the alias's
conjunctions, they are replaced by the leaves its
.Cm apply
produces.
A rule written against the user's value is thus checked against the stored
fields.
Aliases are substituted in order.
.Pp
Conversely, a rule conjunction that does not mention the alias value, but is
satisfied by the alias's expansion alone, does not fire on that exact
expansion.
In the example below, the rule that
.Ql dynamic_range
may only be set while
.Ql dynamic_range_priority
is
.Cm off
would otherwise reject
.Cm hdr800_plus ,
which is stored as
.Cm hdr800
with priority
.Cm plus ;
it still rejects
.Cm hdr800
with priority
.Cm strong .
If codegen cannot decide whether an expansion satisfies a conjunction, because
an ordered comparison involves a non-numeric value, it prints a cargo warning
naming the rule, and the alias value may be rejected at run time.
.Pp
When a render profile is read, aliases are undone, in reverse order, so that
the user sees the alias value.
This requires a
.Cm when
that is a single current-scope
.Cm equals ,
an
.Cm apply
that only sets values, and no other transformation that sets the same fields
to the same values.
Codegen prints a cargo warning for each render transformation that cannot be
undone.
Simulation transformations are not undone.
.Sh COMMAND LINE
Each option without
.Cm codegen.skip
becomes a flag named after its id, with underscores replaced by hyphens, for
example
.Fl -film-simulation
for
.Ql film_simulation .
Its
.Cm category
is the flag's heading in help output.
Simulation commands take a flag for every such option; render commands take one
for every such option used as a render field.
A flag's value is parsed as the option's type: an alias for an
.Cm enum ,
a number or string otherwise, and checked against the option's rules.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa fml/option.cue
.Ql #Option
and all options.
.It Pa fml/camera.cue
.Ql #Camera
and all cameras.
.It Pa fml/generation.cue
.Ql #Generation
and all generations.
.It Pa fml/grammar.cue
Predicates, assignments, rules and transformations.
.It Pa fml/main.cue
The definition root.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Validate the schema and inspect the exported document:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cue vet ./fml
$ cue export ./fml --out json | jq '.options.highlight_tone'
.Ed
.Pp
A float option, stored in tenths:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
highlight_tone: #Option & {
    spec: {
        name:     "Highlight Tone"
        category: "Tone"
        kind:     "float"
        rules: {min: -2.0, max: 4.0, step: 0.5}
        encoding: {
            prop_code: 0xD19D
            kind:      "scale"
            spec: scale: 10
        }
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
An enum option that the camera may refuse to set:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lens_modulation_optimizer: #Option & {
    codegen: flaky: true
    spec: {
        name:     "Lens Modulation Optimizer"
        category: "Lens"
        kind:     "enum"
        rules: variants: [
            {id: "on", name: "On", aliases: ["on", "true"]},
            {id: "off", name: "Off", aliases: ["off", "false"]},
        ]
        encoding: {
            prop_code: 0xD1A3
            kind:      "lookup"
            spec: values: {on: 0x01, off: 0x02}
        }
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A camera that takes its simulation from its generation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
x_s20: #Camera & {
    spec: {
        name:        "FUJIFILM X-S20"
        generation:  "x_trans_iv"
        _generation: _

        usb: product_id: 0x02f7

        features: {
            backup: true
            simulation: {
                slots:    4
                settings: _generation._simulation.settings
                rules:    _generation._simulation.rules
            }
        }
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A rule against a white balance temperature outside Temperature mode.
To repair it, fujicli clears the temperature or, if the user set the temperature, selects
Temperature mode.
The temperature is read only in Temperature mode.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    message: "White balance temperature is only meaningful in Temperature mode."
    when: all: [
        {ref: "white_balance_temperature", present: true},
        {not: {ref: "white_balance", equals: "temperature"}},
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A render alias for a value that the camera stores as two fields:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    when: {ref: "dynamic_range", equals: "hdr800_plus"}
    apply: [
        {ref: "dynamic_range", value: "hdr800"},
        {ref: "dynamic_range_priority", value: "plus"},
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A render rule on the value the image was shot with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    message: "Teleconverter can only be enabled if it was enabled when the image was shot."
    when: all: [
        {ref: "teleconverter", scope: "original", equals: "off"},
        {ref: "teleconverter", equals: "on"},
    ]
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fujicli 1 ,
.Xr fujicli 7
